WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.500
Hey, welcome to Models and Agents for Beginners, Episode 84 for June 26, 2026.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:10.140
Let's break down today's coolest AI news so anyone can understand it.

00:00:10.800 --> 00:00:15.680
Today's AI news is pretty exciting, and I promised no jargon. Let's go.

00:00:16.180 --> 00:00:20.860
Have you ever noticed how playing the same video game for hours makes certain moves feel automatic?

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:27.280
A company called General Intuition just raised money to train entire AI systems on

00:00:27.280 --> 00:00:31.380
millions of hours of that kind of gameplay instead of real-world footage.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.920
The goal is to capture patterns of movement, quick choices, and teamwork that show up across

00:00:36.920 --> 00:00:42.500
games and then transfer those patterns to everyday tasks. Think of it like watching expert

00:00:42.500 --> 00:00:48.120
skateboarders for weeks until your own balance improves, except the AI never gets tired and can

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:55.280
review every trick at once. Another way to picture it is a library where every book is a recorded match,

00:00:55.280 --> 00:01:00.560
and the AI reads them all to learn the rules of motion and timing without ever leaving the

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:06.380
shelf. This approach matters because many future helpers will need to understand physical actions,

00:01:06.900 --> 00:01:12.580
not just answer questions about facts. Students interested in game design or robotics could

00:01:12.580 --> 00:01:18.260
explore these ideas without buying expensive equipment since the training happens inside digital

00:01:18.260 --> 00:01:24.200
worlds that act like safe practice arenas. Right now, there is no public demo available,

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:29.640
but you can explore similar ideas by heading to the hugging face website, searching for game

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:35.580
navigation models, and trying their free examples that show how game data teaches basic movement.

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:41.460
That brings us to the deep dive, where we look under the hood at exactly how this kind of training

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:48.140
works. Start by imagining you already know how to spot patterns in a game like Minecraft without

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:54.060
thinking about it. The AI begins the same way, except it watches millions of recorded matches

00:01:54.060 --> 00:02:01.140
instead of playing once. Step 1 is noticing tiny details such as how fast characters turn or when

00:02:01.140 --> 00:02:07.160
teams coordinate. Step 2 is practicing those details inside digital copies of the game worlds,

00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:14.080
running millions of variations until the useful ones become habits. Step 3 is testing those habits

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:19.860
in brand new situations that AI has never seen before, which is where the quick thinking starts to

00:02:19.860 --> 00:02:26.540
feel like real intuition. The habits can then move to non-game tasks, such as guiding a robot or

00:02:26.540 --> 00:02:32.160
planning a route through a crowded space. By the end of the process, the system has borrowed the

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:38.800
same fast reactions you already used while playing, just scaled up across far more examples than any

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:44.360
single person could experience. Now for some cool stuff you can try yourself. Researcher Ethan

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:52.600
Mollick shared a single prompt that makes models like GLM 5.2 or Opus 4.8 reveal their step-by-step

00:02:52.600 --> 00:02:59.580
reasoning instead of jumping straight to an answer. The prompt asks the AI to suggest two poems that

00:02:59.580 --> 00:03:05.660
fit the current state of AI models, but only after thinking hard and skipping anything popular it could

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:12.360
just copy. Most chatbots hide how they reach a conclusion, so watching the process helps you see why

00:03:12.360 --> 00:03:18.720
certain ideas get chosen. Go to any chatbot that supports these models, paste the exact words,

00:03:19.280 --> 00:03:25.120
I want you to suggest two poems that you think apply very well to the current state of gen AI models

00:03:25.120 --> 00:03:31.360
like you. Don't just pick popular poems and back justify. Think hard about options first,

00:03:31.360 --> 00:03:36.600
and notice the thinking trace that appears before the final answer. In another update,

00:03:36.960 --> 00:03:42.100
notion decided to stop building a new email app because users are already turning to AI

00:03:42.100 --> 00:03:48.840
agents that read, sort, and reply automatically. These agents learn your habits and handle routine

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:54.600
messages so you spend less time clicking through inboxes. For anyone juggling school, clubs,

00:03:54.700 --> 00:04:00.220
and friends, that shift frees up time for creative work instead of sorting messages. You can try

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:07.120
similar agent style help right now by going to notion.oso, opening any page or database and asking

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:13.440
the built-in AI to organize information the way an inbox agent would. Here are a few quick bits worth

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:20.700
knowing. FIFA is giving every world cup team the same AI agent. This year's tournament includes

00:04:20.700 --> 00:04:27.300
an official AI helper any team can use for strategy and analysis. The goal is to keep competition

00:04:27.300 --> 00:04:33.580
fair even if some teams have bigger budgets. Every team gets access to the same tool from FIFA.

00:04:34.180 --> 00:04:40.140
It supports both strategy planning and game analysis during the event. The source for this update is

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:47.680
a story on wired.com. This levels the playing field across all participating nations. A new roadmap

00:04:47.680 --> 00:04:54.540
